NAME
       pvcreate - make a disk or partition into an LVM physical volume

SYNOPSIS
       pvcreate [OPTIONS] DEVICE...

DESCRIPTION
       pvcreate writes an LVM label to a block device, making it a physical
       volume (PV). A PV is the raw material of LVM: you then add PVs to a
       volume group (vgcreate, vgextend) and carve logical volumes out of the
       group (lvcreate).

       A DEVICE can be a whole disk with no partition table (/dev/vdc) or a
       partition (/dev/vdb1). For a partition, setting its type to Linux LVM
       first is good practice (parted: set N lvm on; fdisk: type 8e or
       "Linux LVM"; gdisk: 8e00), though LVM does not require it. A disk
       that has a partition table cannot be used whole.

       If the device already holds a file system or another signature,
       pvcreate asks before wiping it. It refuses a device that is mounted
       or that already belongs to a volume group.

       On RHEL 9 LVM uses a devices file, /etc/lvm/devices/system.devices:
       pvcreate adds the new PV to it, and LVM commands only look at devices
       listed there.

       vgcreate and vgextend run pvcreate themselves when they are given a
       device that is not yet a PV, so this step is often optional.

OPTIONS
       -f, --force
              Do not ask before wiping an existing signature. Given twice
              (-ff) it even reinitialises a PV that belongs to a volume
              group (this destroys that group's data).

       -y, --yes
              Answer yes to every question.

       -v, --verbose
              Say more about what is done.

       -u, --uuid UUID
              Give the PV this UUID (used when restoring LVM metadata).

EXAMPLES
       Make a partition and a whole disk into PVs:

              # parted -s /dev/vdb mklabel gpt mkpart lvm1 1MiB 2049MiB set 1 lvm on
              # pvcreate /dev/vdb1
                Physical volume "/dev/vdb1" successfully created.
              # pvcreate /dev/vdc

       Check them, then use them for a new volume group:

              # pvs
              # vgcreate datavg /dev/vdb1 /dev/vdc

       Grow an existing group with a new PV:

              # pvcreate /dev/vdb2
              # vgextend datavg /dev/vdb2

